WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.560
Cloud identity and access management, or IAM,

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:07.060
is one of the most important security boundaries in a cloud environment.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:11.680
In traditional infrastructure, many teams focused first on network location:

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.260
which subnet a system was in, which firewall was in front of it,

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.320
and who could reach it over the network.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:21.780
Those controls still matter, but cloud environments add a powerful control plane.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.280
A principal with cloud API access may be able to create resources, attach storage,

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:32.780
change policies, modify network rules, rotate keys, deploy workloads,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.800
or delete systems without ever logging into a server.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:40.420
That is why IAM cannot be treated as an administrative afterthought.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.120
A broadly privileged identity can have the practical effect of full infrastructure

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:46.220
control.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.600
In AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, or any similar platform,

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.980
the console and API are how teams build and change the environment.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.480
If too many identities have owner, administrator, contributor, wildcard,

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.620
or full-access permissions,

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.080
the cloud account may be only one mistaken change or compromised credential away from

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.740
serious impact.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.800
Cloud IAM also changes how defenders think about exposure.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.900
A private database may not be reachable from the internet,

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:19.560
but an identity with the right permissions may be able to make it public,

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:24.500
export data, attach a new network path, or grant access to another principal.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.840
A workload with a powerful role may reach services that no human user can reach directly.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:34.940
The control plane can become the path of least resistance when IAM is not designed

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.040
carefully.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:38.420
Good IAM work is engineering work.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.600
It includes inventorying identities, assigning owners, defining tasks,

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:48.300
granting least privilege, using federation and MFA, reducing long-lived keys,

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.960
protecting workload identities, logging IAM changes,

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:55.380
reviewing access, and removing stale permissions.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.960
Secure cloud IAM depends on identity lifecycle, permission design, monitoring,

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:03.020
and governance working together.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:07.400
It is one of the foundations that makes every other cloud security control more reliable.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:12.260
A useful mental model is that IAM is the steering wheel for the cloud control plane.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.840
Network segmentation may decide who can reach a service endpoint,

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:21.180
but IAM decides who can create that service, attach a role, change a bucket policy,

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:26.040
rotate a key, modify logging, or grant access to another principal.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:27.780
When IAM is too broad,

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.960
the environment can be changed in ways that bypass the assumptions behind network diagrams

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:34.780
and firewall rules.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:39.640
This is why cloud IAM needs security, platform, operations,

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:42.020
and application teams working together.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.400
Security teams understand risk,

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:47.980
but engineers understand the tasks that identities must perform.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:52.520
Good IAM design turns that knowledge into scoped roles, reviewed permissions,

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.380
monitored changes, and clear ownership.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.840
The result is not slower engineering;

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:02.780
it is safer engineering with fewer surprise privileges and cleaner recovery when something

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:03.960
goes wrong.
